{"id": "sevt_013oJFT48aLvtbkPMfjR5cZM", "content": [{"text": "No, auth isn't the problem. Auth p99 stays at about 50\u201380 ms for the whole window, with no change at 14:31. Its only deploy, 9f1e2d4 at 11:02, was more than 3 hours before the incident.\n\n- **Timing:** checkout deploy `a3f9c21` went out at 14:31:18. Checkout p99 jumped from about 80 ms at 14:30 to 3,858 ms at 14:31. It kept climbing to about 5,000 ms by 14:59. The page time of 14:32 is just when it was noticed.\n- **Diff:** the commit is labelled a readability refactor. It replaced one batched query (`order_id IN (...)`, grouped in memory) with a per-order loop. That loop runs `SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id = ?` once for every open order. This is the N+1 query pattern.\n- **Log:** the per-row query form appears 3,320 times in `app.log`. The batched `IN (?)` form appears 137 times. Before the deploy, queries were batched, for example `IN (?)` at 14:00:01 with 14 ms duration.\n- **Inventory:** its deploy `7b3e8f0` landed at 14:55, well after the latency started, so it isn't the cause.\n\nRolling back `a3f9c21` (or restoring the batched `order_id__in` query) should fix it.\n\n**Root cause:** `a3f9c21` \u2014 the checkout order-summary refactor replaced one batched `order_id IN (...)` query with a per-order `order_id = ?` query inside a loop (N+1), so each checkout summary issues many sequential DB queries and p99 rose from about 80 ms to about 4 s.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T11:37:07.829396Z", "type": "agent.message"}
